=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: super_spine_router_0
diagnosis_text:
The super_spine_router_0 (AS 65000) has an asymmetric BGP routing failure that renders the super-spine tier inoperative for spine_router_0_0. The root cause is BGP best-path router-ID tiebreaking: super_spine_router_0 receives both 10.0.0.0/24 and 10.0.1.0/24 from two peers — spine_router_0_0 (router-id 172.16.0.1) and spine_router_0_1 (router-id 172.16.0.3). Because router-id 172.16.0.1 < 172.16.0.3, super_spine_router_0 selects spine_router_0_0's advertisement as best-path for both prefixes (confirmed by its routing table: both routes installed via eth0 toward spine_router_0_0 only). When super_spine then re-advertises these routes back to spine_router_0_0, their AS-paths both contain AS 65100 — spine_router_0_0's own AS number — triggering standard BGP loop prevention. spine_router_0_0 drops all 2 received prefixes from super_spine (BGP neighbor statistics show "AS-PATH loop: 2"; FRR logs confirm the DENIED messages at 13:19:46). As a result, spine_router_0_0 receives zero usable routes from the super-spine tier, and super_spine_router_0's eth1 link toward spine_router_0_1 carries no routed traffic. Current inter-rack connectivity (pc_0_0 ↔ pc_0_1) is functional via the direct leaf→spine→leaf path that bypasses super_spine entirely. The fix is to configure `bgp bestpath as-path multipath-relax` and `maximum-paths 2` on super_spine_router_0 so it installs both spine paths and can advertise the spine_router_0_1 path (AS-path 65000 65101 …) back to spine_router_0_0 — a path that does not contain 65100 and would be accepted.
=== END ===